  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nFairly strong shortwave moving through Manitoba will help push a\nsurface trough and cold front down into ND and MN tonight. The\nfront should be past all but our far southeastern counties by\ntomorrow morning, with weak surface high pressure building in\nbehind the boundary for Friday. Northwesterly flow aloft for\nSaturday, with a few of the ensemble members bringing a weak\nembedded shortwave and breaking out some precipitation. CAPE\nvalues from the ensemble average out to around 600 J/kg so if\nanything does develop severe impacts are not expected.\nNorthwesterly flow aloft with some additional weak embedded\nshortwaves moving through continues into the upcoming work week.\nCurrently the best probabilities for precipitation over a tenth\nof an inch is Sunday night/Monday, but timing and placement could\nchange with such minor shortwaves. \n\n...Shower and thunderstorm chances this afternoon and night...\n\nModels continue to show a narrow band of instability out ahead\nof the cold front this afternoon across southeastern ND into\nwestern MN. SPC meso analysis has about 500 J/kg, and with a few\nmore hours of heating ML CAPE could get into the quadruple\ndigits. Shear is around 30 kts, but a weak shortwave could get a\nfew cells going that could produce some lightning, brief \ndownpours, and even a bit of small hail or gusts to 50 mph. The \ncold front will drop through later tonight, with some additional\nelevated thunderstorms possible but most of the CAMs keep \nprecipitation post frontal and unimpressive.",
